.IX Title "Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer 3pm"
.TH Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer \- Multiple Analyzers in series.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 9
\&    my $schema = Lucy::Plan::Schema\->new;
\&    my $polyanalyzer = Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer\->new( 
\&        analyzers => \e@analyzers,
\&    );
\&    my $type = Lucy::Plan::FullTextType\->new(
\&        analyzer => $polyanalyzer,
\&    );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqtitle\*(Aq,   type => $type );
\&    $schema\->spec_field( name => \*(Aqcontent\*(Aq, type => $type );
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
A PolyAnalyzer is a series of Analyzers,
each of which will be called upon to “analyze” text in turn.  You can
either provide the Analyzers yourself, or you can specify a supported
language, in which case a PolyAnalyzer consisting of a
CaseFolder, a
RegexTokenizer, and a
SnowballStemmer will be generated for you.
.PP
The language parameter is DEPRECATED. Use
EasyAnalyzer instead.
.PP
Supported languages:
.PP
.Vb 10
\&    en => English,
\&    da => Danish,
\&    de => German,
\&    es => Spanish,
\&    fi => Finnish,
\&    fr => French,
\&    hu => Hungarian,
\&    it => Italian,
\&    nl => Dutch,
\&    no => Norwegian,
\&    pt => Portuguese,
\&    ro => Romanian,
\&    ru => Russian,
\&    sv => Swedish,
\&    tr => Turkish,
.Ve
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.IX Header "CONSTRUCTORS"
.SS new
.IX Subsection "new"
.Vb 5
\&    my $tokenizer    = Lucy::Analysis::StandardTokenizer\->new;
\&    my $normalizer   = Lucy::Analysis::Normalizer\->new;
\&    my $stemmer      = Lucy::Analysis::SnowballStemmer\->new( language => \*(Aqen\*(Aq );
\&    my $polyanalyzer = Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer\->new(
\&        analyzers => [ $tokenizer, $normalizer, $stemmer, ], );
.Ve
.PP
Create a new PolyAnalyzer.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBlanguage\fR \- An ISO code from the list of supported languages.
DEPRECATED, use EasyAnalyzer instead.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBanalyzers\fR \- An array of Analyzers.  The order of the analyzers
matters.  Don’t put a SnowballStemmer before a RegexTokenizer (can’t stem whole
documents or paragraphs – just individual words), or a SnowballStopFilter
after a SnowballStemmer (stemmed words, e.g. “themselv”, will not appear in a
stoplist).  In general, the sequence should be: tokenize, normalize,
stopalize, stem.
.SH METHODS
.IX Header "METHODS"
.SS get_analyzers
.IX Subsection "get_analyzers"
.Vb 1
\&    my $arrayref = $poly_analyzer\->get_analyzers();
.Ve
.PP
Getter for “analyzers” member.
.SS transform
.IX Subsection "transform"
.Vb 1
\&    my $inversion = $poly_analyzer\->transform($inversion);
.Ve
.PP
Take a single Inversion as input
and returns an Inversion, either the same one (presumably transformed
in some way), or a new one.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBinversion\fR \- An inversion.
.SH INHERITANCE
.IX Header "INHERITANCE"
Lucy::Analysis::PolyAnalyzer isa Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer isa Clownfish::Obj.
